1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,520
Urns began arriving at a German
prisoner-of-war camp.

2
00:00:04,040 --> 00:00:07,360
They held the ashes of Allied airmen who
had escaped through a

3
00:00:07,400 --> 00:00:09,280
tunnel dug under the camp fence.

4
00:00:09,760 --> 00:00:13,760
The German government said the men had
been shot resisting capture or

5
00:00:13,920 --> 00:00:17,920
trying to escape again. Police offices in
different parts of the country

6
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:20,400
sent Berlin reports on separate shootings,

7
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:25,840
all giving the same explanation. The
accounts matched so closely that Berlin

8
00:00:25,920 --> 00:00:29,520
ordered them rewritten. Fifty of the men
who made it through that

9
00:00:29,600 --> 00:00:34,240
tunnel were killed. The escape began on
the night of March 24,

10
00:00:34,400 --> 00:00:40,480
1944, at Stalag Luft Three, a German camp
for captured Allied airmen.

11
00:00:40,587 --> 00:00:43,680
The prisoners had been digging in secret
for months.

12
00:00:43,787 --> 00:00:47,360
They used boards from their beds to hold
up the tunnel walls.

13
00:00:47,440 --> 00:00:51,360
Empty milk tins became pipes for pumping
air underground.

14
00:00:51,440 --> 00:00:54,800
About two hundred men were supposed to get
out that night.

15
00:00:54,880 --> 00:00:58,240
Then the first man came up through the
exit and found that

16
00:00:58,320 --> 00:01:00,640
the tunnel stopped short of the woods.

17
00:01:00,720 --> 00:01:03,600
Anyone who came out had open ground to
cross,

18
00:01:03,680 --> 00:01:07,440
within sight of the camp. The prisoners
used a rope to signal

19
00:01:07,520 --> 00:01:09,680
back to the tunnel when the way was clear.

20
00:01:09,760 --> 00:01:12,000
By the time the guards stopped the escape,

21
00:01:12,160 --> 00:01:16,560
seventy-six men had got out. Only three
made it to safety.

22
00:01:16,640 --> 00:01:20,080
The other seventy-three were recaptured,
and for some of the men in

23
00:01:20,160 --> 00:01:23,680
custody, the next trip would be in a car
with the Gestapo,

24
00:01:23,787 --> 00:01:28,320
the Nazi secret police. Hitler ordered the
execution of dozens of the

25
00:01:28,440 --> 00:01:33,200
recaptured men. Instructions went out from
Berlin to local police offices.

26
00:01:33,307 --> 00:01:36,800
The police were to turn over selected
prisoners to the Gestapo,

27
00:01:36,880 --> 00:01:41,520
supposedly for questioning. The
instructions also gave the story the
officers were

28
00:01:41,600 --> 00:01:46,240
to tell afterward. The prisoners had been
shot trying to get away.

29
00:01:46,320 --> 00:01:49,280
Some of the men who were eventually sent
back to camp had

30
00:01:49,400 --> 00:01:51,440
seen what happened in one of the jails.

31
00:01:51,520 --> 00:01:54,160
Officials picked men out from their cells.

32
00:01:54,240 --> 00:01:58,080
The men selected were led away in
handcuffs and driven off under

33
00:01:58,160 --> 00:02:02,160
armed guard. The prisoners who returned to
camp lived to tell the

34
00:02:02,360 --> 00:02:06,720
British what they’d seen. The Gestapo took
prisoners out in small groups

35
00:02:06,800 --> 00:02:08,960
and killed them at different locations.

36
00:02:09,040 --> 00:02:12,720
The bodies were cremated. Urns were sent
back to the camp,

37
00:02:12,827 --> 00:02:16,800
and reports went to Berlin. After Britain
made the deaths public,

38
00:02:17,120 --> 00:02:20,400
Berlin told the local offices to rewrite
their accounts.

39
00:02:20,480 --> 00:02:24,800
Swiss representatives who monitored the
treatment of prisoners of war were
expected

40
00:02:24,880 --> 00:02:27,440
to ask for details. After the war,

41
00:02:27,520 --> 00:02:31,360
investigators from Britain’s Royal Air
Force went looking for the men who’d

42
00:02:31,440 --> 00:02:34,960
carried out the killings. One case took
them back to the spring

43
00:02:35,040 --> 00:02:39,520
of 1944, when two cars left a town near
the Danish border.

44
00:02:39,920 --> 00:02:42,240
They were carrying four recaptured airmen.

45
00:02:42,800 --> 00:02:46,640
One was James Catanach, an Australian
pilot who’d made it through the

46
00:02:46,720 --> 00:02:49,680
tunnel. He was the only prisoner in the
first car.

47
00:02:50,080 --> 00:02:51,680
The other three were in the second.

48
00:02:52,080 --> 00:02:55,520
The drivers had orders to keep the road
clear of passersby.

49
00:02:55,960 --> 00:02:59,680
Catanach’s car got there first. He was
taken through a gate into

50
00:02:59,760 --> 00:03:02,960
a field and shot. When the second car
arrived,

51
00:03:03,200 --> 00:03:06,320
the three men were led into the same field
and killed.

52
00:03:06,800 --> 00:03:08,720
The drivers stayed by their cars.

53
00:03:09,480 --> 00:03:11,440
Later, when the report had to be
rewritten,

54
00:03:11,760 --> 00:03:13,760
the officers proposed a new version.

55
00:03:14,200 --> 00:03:17,360
It said the two drivers had each shot a
prisoner who tried

56
00:03:17,440 --> 00:03:20,400
to run. Both drivers refused to sign.

57
00:03:20,800 --> 00:03:23,840
Their commander sent a revised report to
Berlin anyway.

58
00:03:24,480 --> 00:03:27,920
After the war, two other men who’d been
there admitted firing at

59
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:31,920
the prisoners. The men involved disagreed
about who’d fired some of the

60
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:35,440
shots. At the trial, the question would be
what each of them

61
00:03:35,520 --> 00:03:38,800
had done, from giving the orders to
driving the cars.

62
00:03:39,280 --> 00:03:44,240
In 1947, eighteen men faced a British
military court in Hamburg.

63
00:03:44,720 --> 00:03:47,960
One was Max Wielen, the head of the
criminal police in the

64
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:51,640
region where the camp stood. He’d helped
direct the search after the

65
00:03:51,680 --> 00:03:55,440
escape. The court heard that he’d been
shown the killing order before

66
00:03:55,520 --> 00:03:58,400
prisoners in his region were handed over
to the Gestapo.

67
00:03:58,880 --> 00:04:03,520
Wielen alone was convicted on the charges
covering the wider killing operation.

68
00:04:03,627 --> 00:04:05,840
He was sentenced to life in prison.

69
00:04:05,947 --> 00:04:10,080
The other seventeen were convicted for
their roles in specific murders.

70
00:04:10,187 --> 00:04:13,600
The two drivers argued that they’d only
driven the cars.

71
00:04:13,707 --> 00:04:16,880
The question for the court was whether
they had knowingly helped carry

72
00:04:16,960 --> 00:04:20,160
out the killings by bringing the prisoners
to that field.

73
00:04:20,320 --> 00:04:24,000
Both drivers were convicted and sentenced
to ten years in prison.

74
00:04:24,600 --> 00:04:26,320
While the war was still going on,

75
00:04:26,560 --> 00:04:29,600
the urns had been placed in a stone
memorial in the woods

76
00:04:29,680 --> 00:04:33,120
outside the camp. The prisoners tended it
for as long as they

77
00:04:33,200 --> 00:04:34,960
remained at Stalag Luft Three.
